### Quadratic Transformation Formulas

If, and only if the numbers \(\pm(1-c)\), \(\pm(a-b)\), \(\pm(a+b-c)\) are such, that two of them are equal or one of them is equal to \(\frac{1}{2}\), then there exists a quadratic transformation. The basic formulas are due to Kummer [15.7] and a complete list is due to Goursat [15.3]. See also [15.2].

**15.3.15**: _F(a, b; 2b; z)=\((1-z)^{-a}F\left(\frac{1}{2}a,b-\frac{1}{2}a;\,b+\frac{1}{2};\,\frac{z^{2}}{4z-4}\right)\)_
**15.3.16**: _=\((1-\frac{1}{2}z)^{-a}F(\frac{1}{2}a,\frac{1}{2}+\frac{1}{2}a;b+\frac{1}{2};z^{2}(2-z)^{-2})\)_
**15.3.17**: _=\((\frac{1}{2}+\frac{1}{2}\sqrt{1-z})^{-a}F\left[a,a-b+\frac{1}{2};\,b+\frac{1}{2};\,\frac{(1-\sqrt{1-z})^{T}}{1+\sqrt{1-z}}\right]\)_
